.TH POST 1
.SH NAME
post \- post a directory of the name space for any other to mount
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B post
.I spec dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Post
posts
.BI #s spec /fs
.RI ( post9p (2))
and serves each open of it as a connection of its own, by exporting
.I dir
as
.IR post 's
name space has it
.RI ( sys-export (2)).
So a server mounted in one name space, or in one Dis virtual machine
.RI ( vm (1)),
can be mounted from any other, with no option of the server's own for
it.
A
.I spec
whose
.B fs
is already posted is refused.
.PP
.I Post
runs until killed, in a process group of its own.
Its connections pass through it (the srv device relays them, as it does
for every posted server), so killing it ends them too.
.SH EXAMPLES
A recorded video served in VM 1 and played in VM 0:
.IP
.EX
vm 1 sh -c 'mount {vid9p clip.y4m} /mnt/video; post video /mnt/video'
mount -A '#svideo/fs' /mnt/video
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/post.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vm (1),
.IR export (4),
.IR post9p (2)
.SH BUGS
Every message passes through an export as well as the relay, so reading
through
.I post
costs more than reading the server directly: about half the rate of a
clip read as fast as it can be, hosted and under QEMU; for a camera on
the Raspberry Pi 4 (30 frames a second of 280 KB), the full rate, for
2.3 points of the processors' time against 12 read directly.
